  "text_md": ":\n\n![](<11_pg418_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<11_pg418_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n# TRO 890 TRO\n\n## TROEN.\n\nand the yarn is pressed by the hand of the\n\nPeny rydys iroen be tioen,\n\nspinner. Forhy.\n\nOvyr all in ylke a toen.\n\n(2) The skinnv part of roasted meat, which be-\n\nOn land and eke on flode.\n\nfore the whole can be dressed becomes tough\n\nReliq. Antiq. ii. 110.\n\nand dry, like a trip overkept, or the leather used by the old woman. Forby.\n\nTROFELYTE. Ornamented with knots. Gaw.\n\nTROGH. A tree.\n\nTRISE. To pull up.\n\nTROGHTE. Belief?\n\nTRISTE. (1)\n\nThe thryde es for-thy that we have Alle o troghte that sal us save.\n\nHast thou be prowde and eke of port For frys^eof lady and eke of lord.\n\nMS. Harl. 2260, f. 21.\n\nM8. Cott. Claud. A. ii. f. 140.\n\nTROIFLARDES. Triflers ; idlers.\n\n- (2) To trust. I was in prison wel ye wist,\n\nTo helpe of you ne myght I tHste.\n\nMS. Addit. 11305, f. 90. My lorde, when he went to the see. For specyalle ti-yste he toke me to the.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 72.\n\n- (3) A post or station in hunting. I se huntynge, I se homes blow,\n\nHoundes renne, the dere drawe adowne. And atte her triste bowes set arow,\n\nNow in August this lusti fressh cesone.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. i. 6, f. 13.\n\n- (4) A trestle, or support.\n- (5) A windlass.\n- (6) A cattle-market. North. TRISTER. See Triste (3). TRISTESCE. Sadness.\n\n\nTROITE. The cuttle-fish ? \" Sepia, Anglice a\n\ntroite,\" Nominale MS.\n\nTROJAN. A boon companion ; a person who is fond of liquor. A cant term. According to some, a thief was so called ; but it was applied\n\nsomewhat indiscriminately. A rough manly\n\nboy is now termed \" a fine Trojan.\" Grose\n\nhas trusty Trojan, a true friend.\n\nTROKE. (1) To barter ; to truck. North.\n\n(2) To fall short.\n\nHe mone stond faste thereby.\n\nOr ellys hys schote woUe troke.\n\nMS. Porkington 10, f. 58\n\nTROKES. Square pieces of wood at the tops\n\nof masts to put the flag-staffs in.\n\nTROLL. To trundle. To troll the howl, to\n\npass the vessel about in drinking. TROLLEN. To draw ; to drag. \\A..S.) TROLL-MADAM. A game borrowed from the\n\nSave only that I crye and bidde,\n\nI am in tristesce alle amidde.\n\nGoiver, MS, Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 136.\n\nFrench in the 16th century, now known under the name of trunks, q. v. Brand quotes a\n\nsecurely. \" Qwhenne they\n\nTRISTILY. Safely ;\n\ntristily had tretyd,\" Morte Arthure, MS. Lin-\n\ncurious account of this game, from which it appears to have formed a favorite indoor amusement with the lady fashionables at Buxton about the year 1572, and to have been somewhat like the modern game of bagatelle. There is an allusion to it in the Winter's Tale,\n\ncoln, f. 57.\n\nTRISTIVE. Sad. {Lat.)\n\nTRISTUR. The same as THste (3).\n\nTRIUMPH. (1) A public show.\n\n(2) A trump at cards, (fr.) The game of\n\ntrump was also so called.\n\niv. 2.\n\nTRIVANT. A truant ; a loiterer. TRIVET. Right as a trivet, perfectly right.\n\nTROLLOP. (1) A slattern. Far. dial.\n\n(2) A string of horses. Line.\n\nA common phrase.\n\nTROLLOPISH. Filthy ; dirty. South.\n\nTRIVIGANT. Termagant. {Ital.)\n\nTROLLY. A low heavy cart. Var. dial.\n\nTRIWEDE. Honesty. Hearve.\n\nTROLLYBAGS. Tripe. Var. dial.\n\nTROACHER. A dealer in smuggled goods. TROANT. A foolish fellow. Exm. TROAT. To bellow, said of the buck. TROCHE. To branch. (A.-N.)\n\nTROLLY-LOLLY. Coarse lace. TROLUBBER. A hedger and ditcher. Devon.\n\nTROME. Band, or company. {A.-S.)\n\nTROMPE. (1) A trumpet. (^.-iV.)\n\nTROCHES. Were thus made\n\n(2) A shin, or shank. TROMPOUR. A trumpeter.\n\nTake of Benjamin six ounces, wood of aloes eight ounces, styrax calamite three ounces, musk half a dram, orrice two ounces, sugar-candy three pound • powder them, and with rose-water make\n\nTRONAGE. A toll for the weighing ofwool in\n\nthe market. Coles.\n\nTRONCHEON. A scab.\n\ntroches. Cosmeticks, 1660. p. 138.\n\nTRONCHON. A fragment. {A.-N.)\n\nTROCHINGS. The cluster of small branches\n\nat the top of a stag's horn.\n\nUpon a tronchon of a spere, He set the hed of the bore.\n\nTROD. A footpath. Line. \" Ran from trod\n\nto trod,\" Du Bartas, p. 360.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 100.\n\nTRONE. (1) A throne. {A.-N.) It is the verb,\n\nTRODE. Track ;\n\npath. (A.-S.)\n\nYf thou ever trowyde ore uudyrstode That thi wytt ore thi gude\n\nto enthrone, in this example. And ther soulys to hevyn here. Before God tronyd they were.\n\nCommys of thiselfe and nojte of Code, That es grctt pryde and fals trode.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 38.\n\nR. de Brunne, MS. Bowes, p. 16. TRODUS. Steps.\n\n- (2) A ridge of mown hay. West.\n- (3) A post, or log of wood. TRONES. A steelyard. North. TRONSOUN. A club, or staflF.\n\n\nThey nyste never wher he was a-go, Ne of his trodus no sygne ther nasse.\n\nCh)vn. Vitodun, p. 15.\n\n",
